From: DEREK@alcor.org
Date:  Thu, 22 Sep 94 20:44:04 
Subject: [#94-9-395] Wired, and Other Moving Experiences

First, I'll throw in my "Me, too!" on the Wired article, which I just 
read this afternoon.

Journalistic objectivity indeed.  In less than five magazine pages, Ed 
used *thirty-eight* exclamation points! (!!!)  I think he likes us.  :-)

I for one am glad to finally see an article about extropianism that 
doesn't waffle.

Also, at the risk of sounding like a religious zealot, (please tune out 
if "sharing" bothers you), I'll go ahead and mention that while I was 
reading the article my adrenaline raced very high.  (Upon finishing, 
I felt the uncontrollable urge to go do some push-ups!)  Though I 
have read synopses of the Extropian Principles and heard 
expositions on the Favorite Extropian Topics many times over the 
past four years, I still get a lump in my throat any time I read (or 
hear) someone discussing our prospects for the future in such an 
enthusiastic manner.

[Has anyone here ever heard Max read Nietzsche in his Uber 
Voice?  It's great fun at parties, and I highly recommend it to the 
uninitiated.]

And this brings up a phenomenon which puzzles me somewhat.  
I've noticed that I have come to a point in my life where I only get 
extremely emotional (i.e., to the point of tears, or nearly to the 
point of tears--whether motivated by joy or sorrow) when I 
experience some moving piece of art or literature. . . Movies, 
books, or music.  In normal life, rarely do I experience anything so 
out of the ordinary that I simply must cry or scream out.

But when I read Vernor Vinge (Heinlein and Rand when I was a bit 
younger), or listen to Michael Brecker, or watch a life-affirming 
movie like "The Doctor", I am sometimes so overwhelmed that I 
could just burst.  This is how I felt for a few moments while reading 
the Wired article. . .  like something very extropic--something 
*major*--was happening in the universe.  All at once the realization 
that *we are making progress* hit me, and it felt very, very good.

I see two things that differentiate these experiences from normal 
life.  First, they are much more condensed.  I get to segue through a 
wide spectrum of different brain states (very much like riding a 
roller coaster) in a short period of time.  This what I love about 
Vinge so much.  He lets me view a rapid personal growth 
phase--a sort of "individual singularity"--for each of his characters.  
I get to vicariously experience that which I desire for my own life 
over the *long term* in just a few hundred pages.  It's very 
satisfying.

Second, these works are idealized.  You get maximum tension 
leading to maximum relief.

What still confuses me is that, in normal life, I go to great lengths to 
control my brain states for maximum productivity and happiness.  
That is, I've learned that staying on a more or less even keel gets me 
the most of what I want out of life.  But when I am in the mood to 
read fiction, or watch a great movie, or listen to some powerful 
music, I *seek* these extremes.  I want to be moved to the point of 
tears.  It feels very good, very extropian.

Am I the only one like this?  Could it be my now outmoded 
religious meme complexes that loved altar call so much when I was 
an adolescent?  Do other extropians seek out such experiences and 
value them as I do?  

Forward in all directions!

Derek Ryan
derek@alcor.org

